\begin_layout Title
Public Economics I: Final Exam Review
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Spring 2012
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question 1
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effect of sentence length on recidivism rates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
There is a discontinuity in expected sentence length depending on the age
 of the perpetrator.
 Criminals under the age of 18 face more lenient penalties than the criminals
 who are over the age of 18.
 However, this would be a fuzzy RD because there are minors who can be tried
 as adults.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, we have the following setup:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Running Variable: Age
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Cutoff: 18 years
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Treatment group: Individuals older than 18
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Control group: Individuals younger than 18
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Source of variation: Individuals under the age of 18 receive shorter sentences
 than adults
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Outcome: Recidivism (binary variable)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
Covariates: Individual sentence length, type of crime, date of birth of
 the criminal
\end_layout

\begin_layout Itemize
First-stage: The effect of age on expected sentence length
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
All other determinants that could affect the outcome are smooth across the
 cutoff; there is no manipulation around the cutoff.
 The behavior that would violate the assumpions in part A if people under
 18 are more inclined to commit crimes because of the more lenient penalties
 they face.
 To test the identifying assumption, we test to see if there are any discontinui
ties in the other observable determinants that could affect recidivism.
 We do this by showing the distribution of these determinants and the estimates
 of the reduced-form regressions in which the determinant is placed on the
 left hand side of the equation.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $T_{i}=1(\mbox{age}_{i}\geq18)$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}=\mbox{age}_{i}-18$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=\mbox{recidivism}_{i}$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
y_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}x_{i}+\beta_{2}x_{i}^{2}+\beta_{3}T_{i}+\beta_{4}T_{i}\cdot x_{i}+\beta_{5}T_{i}\cdot x_{i}^{2}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

The variable of interest is 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{3}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we are able to discern all of the noncompliers, then it would be better
 to drop them from the data set.
 However, we are unable to completely discern all of the noncompliers in
 this example, so it would not be wise to drop the observable noncompliers.
 Yes, we can identify the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

always takers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on the left hand side of the cutoff: children under the age of 18 who are
 still tried as an adult.
 Yet, we are unable to identify the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

always takers
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 on the right side of the cutoff: individuals who are over 18 charged as
 an adult who would have still been charged as an adult even if they were
 under the age of 18.
 If we drop the observed noncompliers, then we no longer have random assignment,
 which will cause problems with our estimates.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Question 2
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The effect of prison sentence length on recidivism rates
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Reduced-form IV equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Recidivism}_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}1(\mbox{Initial Assignment to Judge B}_{i})+\beta_{2}X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
where 
\begin_inset Formula $X_{i}$
\end_inset

 are other observable characteristics.
 It is not random whether you actually got Judge A or B, but initial assignment
 was random.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Relevance
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Regress length of sentence on initial assignment and the other observable
 characteristics
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
LS_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}1(\mbox{Initial Assignment to Judge B}_{i})+\beta_{2}X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\beta_{1}=0$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{a}:\beta_{1}\neq0$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $F>10$
\end_inset

, then it is a relevant instrument
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Exogeneity
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Comparison of means test.
 Comparing the group of people who were initially assigned to judge A to
 people who were initially assigned to judge B.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You can also place the indicator of the initial judge assignment on the
 left hand side, and all of the observable characteristics on the right
 hand side.
 Run a regression, then conduct an F test to see whether or not all of the
 coefficients are equal to zero 
\begin_inset Formula $(H_{0}:\beta_{0}=\beta_{1}=\cdots=\beta_{K}=0)$
\end_inset

.
 We hope to fail to reject the null hypothesis.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We can also test whether the instrument is related to observables we know
 to be related to outcomes.
 If it is, then we are concerned that it might be related to unobservables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
You would use the complete sample because that is where your randomization
 is.
 If you throw out the people who were assigned to judge C, then you no longer
 have random assignment.
 Reduced-form IV equation:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Recidivism}_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}1(\mbox{Initial Assignment to Judge B}_{i})+\beta_{2}X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
First-stage:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Sentence Length}_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}1(\mbox{Initial Assignment to Judge B}_{i})+\beta_{2}X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset

Second-stage:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Recidivism}_{i}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\widehat{\mbox{Sentence Length}_{i}}+\beta_{2}X_{i}+\epsilon_{i}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part E
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If we assume that harsher penalties are given to the worse criminals who
 are more likely to recidivate, then the naive OLS estimate will be biased
 upwards on the number line, i.e.,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{\beta}_{OLS}>\hat{\beta}_{2SLS}
\]

\end_inset

Once we separate the part of sentence length that is uncorrelated with the
 error term (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation) from the part that is correlated with the error term (
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation), we isolate the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

good
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation and disregard the 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

bad
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 variation.
 We are able to take away the effect of being assigned to a harsher judge.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Fall 2012
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part A
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
In order to perform the estimation, we must have students who go from no
 bilingual education to bilingual and vice versa (treatment group) and students
 who do not change at all (control group).
 The panel data version of a difference-in-difference framework is
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\mbox{Score}_{it}=\beta_{0}+\beta_{1}\mbox{Year}_{t}+\beta_{2}\mbox{Student}_{i}+\beta_{3}\mbox{Treatment}_{it}+u_{it}
\]

\end_inset

where we control for year and student fixed effects.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Treatment}_{it}=1$
\end_inset

 if 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 receives treatment in Year 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

.
 To make sure inference is performed correctly, I would cluster the standard
 errors at the individual level.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part B
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Students with lower ability could be assigned to bilingual classes, which
 would understate the positive effect that bilingual education has on Hispanic
 students.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part C
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We want to compare the schools who changed bilingual education status (treatment
 group) with schools that did not change (either always offered bilingual
 education or never offered it).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part D
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The schools can hold back or accelerate students in order to manipulate
 the number of limited English proficient students in a particular grade/year
 so as to avoid offering bilingual education.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part E
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I can redefine whether a cohort receives treatment based on the number of
 limited English proficient students in 2003.
 I would then be measuring an intent to treat (ITT) effect.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Part F
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
I can utilize a regression discontinuity design in which the cutoff is 20
 students.
 The running variable would be the predicted number of enrolled limited
 English proficient students after the law takes affect based on figures
 from 2003 (the year before the law took affect).
 This method only works if the schools are unaware of the 20 student cutoff
 before the law is implemented.
\end_layout

